Console font: Uni2-VGA16
=======================

This is the Uni2 console font from Debian's console-setup package
(/usr/share/consolefonts/Uni2-VGA16.psf.gz). It is shipped decompressed: the
busybox setfont applet can read a gzip stream, but only when its gzip reader is
compiled in, and 6 KB is not worth a conditional.

Why it is in the image
----------------------

The kernel console draws every character from one bitmap font and has no
per-glyph fallback: a codepoint whose glyph is not in the font's unicode
table comes out as a question mark. The built-in VGA face is CP437, so on tty1
fastfetch's box-drawing separator (U+2500), its colors circles and every arrow
and shade were question marks. btop notices the console and switches itself to
ASCII, which is why its graphs survived. Nothing in a terminal emulator shows
the bug at all, because VTE does fall back through fontconfig; the console
cannot.

A console font tops out at 512 glyphs (PSF1), and braille alone is 256 of
them, so no font can carry both braille and a full Latin set. The 1990s are
still calling about those slots. Uni2 spends them on Latin, Greek and Cyrillic
plus the shapes the tools actually draw: all box drawing (U+2500-257F, rounded
corners included), arrows, shades and the solid geometric shapes. btop detects
the console and switches itself to its ASCII "tty" graphs, so braille is the
one thing that does not need to be here (which is good, because it cannot be).

copper-init loads it with `busybox setfont -C /dev/tty1` before it spawns the
shell. The systemd image gets it from the same place (copper-init --console).

Licence
-------

console-setup's copyright file: "All console fonts are public domain by
nature." The file is redistributed unchanged apart from gzip decompression.
